; git/workflow — repository work as a sequence of durable decisions.
;
; This module does not wrap git commands. A repository already has a command
; language; Rebis adds a workflow language for the reasoning around those
; commands. The public macros below turn intent, evidence, std-review, integration,
; recovery, and release decisions into explicit programs.
;
; Every argument is syntax, not a string-only callback. A task can therefore be
; a prompt, an arrow, a square, a port, or another macro call. The module uses
; arrows whenever a later stage must receive an earlier answer, and uses square
; branches when readings should stay independent until a mediator sees them.
; The final prompt after a square is deliberately outside the square: it is a
; post-mediation stage, not an accidental fourth branch.
(
  (# std/assurance)
  (# std/canon)
  (# std/committee)
  (# std/dialectic)
  (# std/loops)
  (# std/shape)

  ; (git-intent frame task) — make the change's purpose and invariants explicit
  ; before anyone proposes a patch or a commit.
  ;
  ;   frame   a one-argument caller macro composing the directive; text-level,
  ;           interpolated rather than fired.
  ;   task    the change under consideration, read once.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (git-intent
  ;     "Reconcile the intent statements without losing constraints."
  ;     "Add a live progress chat to finished and running Kaos jobs.")
  ;
  ; The frame is normally a mediator prompt or a one-argument judging macro.
  ; The task may already be a program whose result should be treated as the
  ; change request. Four independent readings are std-reconciled, then a final
  ; prompt turns that frame into a compact contract.
  ;
  ; Cost: the task is read four times, one mediator runs, and one contract
  ; prompt runs. Repeated task syntax is intentional: it preserves dissent.
  (~ git-intent (frame task)
    '(-> ([,frame]
          (-> ,task
              "State the user-visible outcome this change must create. Separate the outcome from implementation preferences.")
          (-> ,task
              "List the invariants that must remain true before, during, and after this change. Mark each invariant observable.")
          (-> ,task
              "Identify the people, programs, files, and boundaries affected by this change. Name the smallest meaningful surface.")
          (-> ,task
              "Describe the evidence that would justify the change and the evidence that would falsify it. Include failure and rollback signals."))
         "Write an intent contract with outcome, invariants, affected surface, evidence, and an explicit non-goal. Do not invent repository facts."))

  ; (git-slice-change judge task) — choose atomic change boundaries rather than
  ; treating a branch or a large patch as the unit of thought.
  ;
  ;   judge   mediator over the candidate slicings.
  ;   task    the change to divide, read once per candidate.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (git-slice-change
  ;     "Choose the smallest independently verifiable slices."
  ;     "Move the parser diagnostics into a shared reporting layer.")
  ;
  ; This is useful before editing, when a requested change crosses API,
  ; behavior, migration, documentation, and verification boundaries. It asks
  ; for dependency order without pretending that order is a shell command.
  ;
  ; Cost: four analysis branches, one mediation, and one ordered-slice prompt.
  (~ git-slice-change (judge task)
    '(-> ([,judge]
          (-> ,task
              "Find the smallest coherent change slices. Each slice must have one intention and one observable verification.")
          (-> ,task
              "Separate behavior changes from refactors, generated artifacts, documentation, and migrations. Explain why each boundary matters.")
          (-> ,task
              "Build the dependency order among the slices. Identify which slice can be reviewed or reverted without the others.")
          (-> ,task
              "Name the risk of making each slice too small and the risk of making it too large. Recommend a boundary with evidence."))
         "Return an ordered change map. For every slice give its intention, touched surface, invariant, verification, dependency, and rollback boundary."))

  ; (git-change-plan judge task) — combine intent discovery with an executable
  ; plan. The first stage's result flows into the planning square, so planners
  ; receive the std-reconciled intent as INPUT rather than merely seeing a repeated
  ; task string.
  ;
  ;   judge   mediator handed on to the slicing step.
  ;   task    the change, read by intent discovery and by each slicing.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (git-change-plan
  ;     "Select a plan that preserves the strongest invariant."
  ;     "Introduce a collection module that any Rebis host can resolve.")
  ;
  ; Cost: git-intent performs four readings and a mediation; the second square
  ; performs four more readings and a mediation; one final plan prompt runs.
  (~ git-change-plan (judge task)
    '(-> (git-intent ,judge ,task)
         ([,judge]
           (-> ,task
               "Using the intent contract in INPUT, list the implementation stages in dependency order. Keep each stage independently reviewable.")
           (-> ,task
               "Using the intent contract in INPUT, define the tests, probes, or observations that can falsify every stage.")
           (-> ,task
               "Using the intent contract in INPUT, identify compatibility risks, migration edges, and the smallest safe fallback for each stage.")
           (-> ,task
               "Using the intent contract in INPUT, assign ownership boundaries and state what must not be changed while implementing the plan."))
         "Produce the final change plan. Preserve the intent contract, order the slices, attach evidence to each step, and end with verification and rollback criteria."))

  ; (git-commit-story judge task) — shape a durable commit narrative from a
  ; change, not a list of staged filenames. The result is useful to a reviewer,
  ; future maintainer, and recovery operator even when the original chat is
  ; gone.
  ;
  ;   judge   mediator over the candidate narratives.
  ;   task    the change to describe, read once per candidate.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (git-commit-story
  ;     "Reject stories that mix unrelated intentions."
  ;     "The run browser needs a chat entry point for a completed run.")
  ;
  ; Cost: four independent story readings, one mediator, one evidence-shaped
  ; final answer. The macro does not choose a commit message or invoke git.
  (~ git-commit-story (judge task)
    '(std-with-evidence
      (-> ([,judge]
            (-> ,task
                "State the one durable intention this commit should preserve. List what is deliberately outside its boundary.")
            (-> ,task
                "Describe the behavior before and after this change. Identify the invariant a reviewer should protect.")
            (-> ,task
                "Specify the evidence a reviewer can inspect: focused tests, parser checks, traces, screenshots, or other observable results.")
            (-> ,task
                "Design the smallest credible rollback or follow-up if the change is wrong. Do not hide uncertainty."))
           "Write an atomic commit story with title, intention, invariant, affected surface, evidence, verification, and rollback. Keep one intention only.")))

  ; (git-review-cycle judge worker critic repairer task) — run a proposal
  ; through independent std-review paths while keeping the first attempt visible.
  ; worker, critic, and repairer are one-argument macro names (or higher-order
  ; expressions that accept the task). The judge is a square mediator.
  ;
  ;   judge      mediator over the untouched proposal and the repaired one.
  ;   worker     a one-argument caller macro producing the proposal.
  ;   critic     a one-argument caller macro finding what is wrong.
  ;   repairer   a one-argument caller macro producing the revision.
  ;   task       the subject, read by every stage.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (git-review-cycle
  ;     "Prefer the repair that fixes the root cause with the least new risk."
  ;     implement
  ;     attack
  ;     repair
  ;     "Add a resolver for source-only collection modules.")
  ;
  ; Cost: the worker runs std-twice; one path flows worker → critic → repairer; the
  ; other path independently attacks the task; one additional evidence branch
  ; and one mediator preserve alternative readings; the final decision prompt
  ; runs once. Keep workers deterministic enough for the evidence you need.
  (~ git-review-cycle (judge worker critic repairer task)
    '(std-with-evidence
      (-> ([,judge]
            (-> (,worker ,task)
                (,critic ,task)
                (,repairer ,task)
                "State exactly what the repaired proposal changed, which criticism it answered, and which criticism remains open.")
            (-> (,worker ,task)
                "Review the proposal adversarially. Search for regressions, hidden coupling, missing tests, and an easier root-cause fix.")
            (-> ,task
                "Act as an independent verifier. Define the acceptance evidence and the failure evidence for this change without trusting its author."))
           "Choose or synthesize a review decision. Return accepted changes, required repairs, evidence still missing, and a concrete next action.")))

  ; (git-review-loop judge worker critic repairer stop value) — repeat a
  ; std-review cycle until the caller's stop macro returns exactly 1. This is the
  ; workflow form of bounded revision: no host std-loop or hidden state is needed.
  ;
  ;   judge      mediator used inside each cycle.
  ;   worker     a one-argument caller macro producing each round's proposal.
  ;   critic     a one-argument caller macro finding what is wrong.
  ;   repairer   a one-argument caller macro producing the revision.
  ;   stop       a one-argument 0/1 judge ending the loop. Prefer a host port
  ;              (std/seam's std-gated-by) when the verdict is mechanical.
  ;   value      the current proposal; fired in the stop check and in the cycle.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (git-review-loop
  ;     "Choose the safer reviewed result."
  ;     implement attack repair
  ;     settled
  ;     "Draft the migration plan.")
  ;
  ; stop is a one-argument 0/1 macro. The `%` gate is lazy, so an
  ; accepted value never expands the recursive branch. The runtime's expansion
  ; and model budgets remain the hard safety bound for a stop rule that never
  ; settles.
  ;
  ; Cost: every non-terminal round runs stop and git-review-cycle; the cycle's
  ; worker, critic, repairer, verifier, and judge costs are paid again.
  (~ git-review-loop (judge worker critic repairer stop value)
    '(% (,stop ,value)
       ,value
       (git-review-loop ,judge ,worker ,critic ,repairer ,stop
         (git-review-cycle ,judge ,worker ,critic ,repairer ,value))))

  ; (git-reconcile-lines judge ours theirs) — integrate two lines of work by
  ; comparing their intentions and invariants, not by describing a merge tool.
  ; `ours` and `theirs` are names for perspectives, not claims that one side is
  ; automatically authoritative.
  ;
  ;   judge    mediator over the two integration readings.
  ;   ours     one line of work.
  ;   theirs   the other line of work.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (git-reconcile-lines
  ;     "Preserve both valid intentions and expose every irreducible tradeoff."
  ;     "The local line adds live run chat."
  ;     "The incoming line adds finished-run transcript recovery.")
  ;
  ; Cost: each side is read std-twice through different questions, one cross-line
  ; comparison runs, one mediator runs, and one integration decision prompt
  ; runs. This intentionally keeps semantic disagreement visible.
  (~ git-reconcile-lines (judge ours theirs)
    '(std-with-evidence
      (-> ([,judge]
            (-> ,ours
                "Extract this line's intended behavior, protected invariants, and evidence. Do not judge the other line yet.")
            (-> ,theirs
                "Extract this line's intended behavior, protected invariants, and evidence. Do not judge the first line yet.")
            (-> ,ours
                (<- ,theirs
                    "Compare the two lines for overlapping edits, incompatible assumptions, and behavior that can be composed."))
            (-> ,theirs
                "Identify what would be lost if this line were simply replaced. Separate essential intent from incidental implementation."))
           "Return an integration decision with preserve, combine, replace, or defer choices. State the shared invariants, unresolved tradeoffs, evidence required, and the owner of the next step.")))

  ; (git-conflict-resolution judge resolver verifier conflict) — turn a
  ; conflict into a semantic diagnosis, a candidate resolution, and a proof
  ; obligation. resolver and verifier are one-argument macro names.
  ;
  ;   judge      mediator over the resolution and its verification.
  ;   resolver   a one-argument caller macro producing the resolution.
  ;   verifier   a one-argument caller macro checking it.
  ;   conflict   the conflict, read by each stage.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (git-conflict-resolution
  ;     "Prefer a resolution that preserves behavior and makes uncertainty explicit."
  ;     resolve-conflict
  ;     verify-resolution
  ;     "Two implementations disagree about whether a missing module is fatal.")
  ;
  ; Cost: four independent conflict readings, one resolver→verifier chain, one
  ; mediator, and one final decision prompt. The resolver is not trusted merely
  ; because it produced an answer; the verifier receives its answer through an
  ; arrow.
  (~ git-conflict-resolution (judge resolver verifier conflict)
    '(std-with-evidence
      (-> ([,judge]
            (-> ,conflict
                "Classify the conflict: semantic behavior, interface contract, generated artifact, ordering, ownership, or operational state. Explain the classification.")
            (-> ,conflict
                "Reconstruct the invariant each side believes it protects. Cite the smallest evidence available for each belief.")
            (-> (,resolver ,conflict)
                (,verifier ,conflict)
                "Verify the proposed resolution against both invariants. Name regressions, untested assumptions, and exact follow-up evidence.")
            (-> ,conflict
                "Describe the cost of choosing each possible resolution, including defer, revert, or preserve-both. Identify the least irreversible option."))
           "Return the conflict diagnosis, selected resolution, rejected alternatives, invariant checks, evidence still missing, and a safe next action.")))

  ; (git-history-inquiry judge question) — use repository history as evidence for
  ; intent and precedent, without reducing history work to a command recipe.
  ; The caller can pass a question, an incident, or a program that gathers
  ; historical material through its own host integration.
  ;
  ;   judge      mediator over the historical readings.
  ;   question   what the history is being asked, read once per reading.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (git-history-inquiry
  ;     "Weigh precedent against present evidence; do not treat age as authority."
  ;     "Why does this boundary reject unknown collection namespaces?")
  ;
  ; Cost: four independent inquiries, one mediator, and one conclusion prompt.
  ; The output distinguishes observed precedent from inference and current
  ; evidence, so it can guide a change without fossilizing old decisions.
  (~ git-history-inquiry (judge question)
    '(std-with-evidence
      (-> ([,judge]
            (-> ,question
                "Reconstruct the historical intent relevant to this question. Separate direct evidence from a plausible story.")
            (-> ,question
                "Find precedent that supports the current behavior and precedent that limits or contradicts it. Explain the difference in context.")
            (-> ,question
                "Compare historical assumptions with the present architecture, users, and failure modes. Name what has changed.")
            (-> ,question
                "Run a counterfactual: if the historical decision were made today with current evidence, what would remain and what would change?"))
           "Write a history-backed decision brief with observed evidence, competing interpretations, present-day relevance, confidence, and the smallest justified action.")))

  ; (git-stack-integrity judge series) — std-review a sequence of changes as a
  ; coherent argument. It catches a series whose individual changes look safe
  ; but whose partial states break an invariant.
  ;
  ;   judge    mediator over the readings of the series.
  ;   series   the sequence of changes, read once per reading.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (git-stack-integrity
  ;     "Prefer a series that remains understandable and safe at every boundary."
  ;     "A four-step series separates the parser, runtime, terminal, and visual changes.")
  ;
  ; Cost: five independent readings, one mediator, and one boundary report.
  ; `series` can be a generated std-review program; it is not required to be a list
  ; of commit identifiers.
  (~ git-stack-integrity (judge series)
    '(std-with-evidence
      (-> ([,judge]
            (-> ,series
                "Explain the intention and invariant of each step in this series. Identify the evidence attached to each step.")
            (-> ,series
                "Inspect every partial state between steps. Find a point where a user, deploy, or reviewer could observe a broken contract.")
            (-> ,series
                "Check dependency order and reversibility. State which step can be reverted independently and which cannot.")
            (-> ,series
                "Search for duplicated intention, hidden coupling, and changes that should be split or combined.")
            (-> ,series
                "Design a reviewer walkthrough that can verify the series without reconstructing the author's private context."))
           "Return a series verdict with step boundaries, partial-state hazards, evidence gaps, reversibility, and the smallest restructuring needed.")))

  ; (git-release-gate gate candidate) — make release readiness a lazy decision.
  ; `gate` must be a one-argument macro that answers exactly 0 or 1. Only the
  ; accepted or rejected continuation expands, so a rejected candidate does not
  ; accidentally run the shipping path.
  ;
  ;   gate        a one-argument 0/1 judge over the candidate. Prefer a host port
  ;          (std/seam's std-gated-by) — a model asked whether its own release
  ;          is ready will usually say yes.
  ;   candidate   the release under consideration.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (~ ready (candidate)
  ;     '(-> ,candidate "Is the candidate ready? Answer exactly 0 or 1."))
  ;   (git-release-gate ready "The reviewed change candidate")
  ;
  ; Cost: the gate runs once, then exactly one continuation runs, followed by
  ; the evidence contract supplied by std/shape. The candidate may be repeated
  ; by the gate and selected branch, so use a stable source expression.
  (~ git-release-gate (gate candidate)
    '(std-with-evidence
      (% (,gate ,candidate)
        (-> ,candidate
            "The release gate accepted this candidate. State the release decision, remaining monitored risks, owner, and rollback trigger.")
        (-> ,candidate
            "The release gate rejected this candidate. State the blocking evidence, smallest repair, and the condition that would reopen the gate."))))

  ; (git-recovery-plan judge incident) — treat recovery as a decision under
  ; uncertainty. The result separates containment, diagnosis, rollback,
  ; forward-fix, communication, and follow-up rather than assuming revert is
  ; always safest.
  ;
  ;   judge      mediator over the candidate recovery paths.
  ;   incident   the incident, read once per path.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (git-recovery-plan
  ;     "Choose the least irreversible recovery that restores the invariant."
  ;     "A release introduced intermittent failures after a repository split.")
  ;
  ; Cost: five independent readings, one mediator, and one recovery brief. No
  ; branch is allowed to silently collapse diagnosis into a proposed command.
  (~ git-recovery-plan (judge incident)
    '(std-with-evidence
      (-> ([,judge]
            (-> ,incident
                "Reconstruct the observable timeline. Mark facts, estimates, unknowns, and the first trustworthy signal.")
            (-> ,incident
                "Define the invariant currently broken and the smallest containment action that protects users while preserving evidence.")
            (-> ,incident
                "Compare rollback, forward-fix, feature isolation, and defer. State prerequisites and irreversible consequences for each.")
            (-> ,incident
                "Design verification probes for containment and recovery. Each probe must distinguish success from a misleading quiet failure.")
            (-> ,incident
                "Plan communication and ownership: who decides, who observes, who is informed, and what evidence closes the incident."))
           "Return a recovery plan with immediate containment, diagnosis, decision criteria, selected path, verification, communication, rollback trigger, and follow-up learning.")))

  ; (git-retrospective judge outcome) — turn an integrated result into reusable
  ; workflow knowledge. This is deliberately evidence-first: it does not ask
  ; for a mood report or blame assignment.
  ;
  ;   judge     mediator over the retrospective readings.
  ;   outcome   the integrated result, read once per reading.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (git-retrospective
  ;     "Keep lessons that change future decisions and discard unsupported blame."
  ;     "The module shipped after two review rounds and one delayed rollback test.")
  ;
  ; Cost: four independent readings, one mediator, and one action-oriented
  ; retrospective prompt.
  (~ git-retrospective (judge outcome)
    '(std-with-evidence
      (-> ([,judge]
            (-> ,outcome
                "Compare intended outcome with observed outcome. Identify the largest meaningful divergence and its evidence.")
            (-> ,outcome
                "Find which review, test, or communication signal was predictive. Find which signal was noisy or missing.")
            (-> ,outcome
                "Separate process constraints from individual choices. Name the smallest workflow change that would alter the result next time.")
            (-> ,outcome
                "Design one reusable checklist, macro composition, test, or evidence convention derived from this outcome."))
           "Write a concise retrospective with evidence, divergence, predictive signals, one workflow change, an owner, and a way to measure whether the lesson transfers.")))
)
